1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:03,360
Rysh doesn't care which
agent you run, or how many.

2
00:00:03,520 --> 00:00:07,680
New stack two, and this lane now
holds three panes in one group.

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:10,320
Stacked, Zellij style: one open,

4
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:13,120
the others collapsed to a title
bar with their number on it.

5
00:00:14,935 --> 00:00:16,135
Now fill them.

6
00:00:16,295 --> 00:00:21,015
Control S and a digit jumps straight to
a pane, so pane one gets Claude Code.

7
00:00:21,015 --> 00:00:23,735
Pane two gets Codex instead
— a different vendor,

8
00:00:23,735 --> 00:00:27,495
a different process, in the same
session. Pane three gets a second Claude.

9
00:01:12,745 --> 00:01:16,345
Each one gets its own instruction,
typed straight into its pane.

10
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:21,160
There is no orchestrator here and
no shared queue — every pane is just

11
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:24,760
an agent, being told what to do by
the person sitting in front of it.

12
00:02:22,835 --> 00:02:27,235
And now flip through the stack.
All three are working at once.

13
00:02:27,475 --> 00:02:29,715
You see one at a time
because they're stacked,

14
00:02:29,715 --> 00:02:32,930
but nothing is waiting its
turn — three agent processes,

15
00:02:33,090 --> 00:02:38,130
each with its own shell and its own
conversation, running side by side underneath.

16
00:02:55,840 --> 00:03:00,000
Three files, written by three
agents, in parallel, in one session.

17
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:02,560
Everything in this video
came out of a single terminal

18
00:03:02,560 --> 00:03:05,280
window and a binary you
install with one command.

